      "class": "page-hero"
    },
    "html": "\n  <div class=\"hero-meta\">\n    <span class=\"level-badge\" data-level=\"4\">Level 4 - Advanced</span>\n    <span class=\"time\">45 min</span>\n    <span class=\"chip accent\">Coding</span>\n    <span class=\"chip\">Deep dive</span>\n    <span class=\"chip\">Dynamic programming</span>\n  </div>\n  <h1>DP State Design: How to Find the Recurrence</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Most people who \"cannot do DP\" can code a table fine. What they cannot do is decide what the table <em>means</em>. This page is about that one skill: choosing the state. We start from a brute-force recursion, read the state straight off its parameters, add a memo, turn it into a table, and then go through the shapes states come in (prefix or suffix, a pair of indices, an interval, an index plus a mode) with a checklist you can run in the first five minutes of an interview. Every template was run against tests (Go pending a compiler).</p>\n"
